  "worked_solution": "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{s}$ with $\\text{min}.$ To get rid of $\\text{s},$ we can put $\\text{s}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{s}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{s}}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{s}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $150\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}} & =150\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =(150\\cdot 60)\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{s}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{s}}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =9\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the space rocket consumes $9\\,000$ kilograms per minute.",
